# The Cab Lab core contract

The Cab Lab is a cabinet CAD, not a general CAD. Follow these rules in every change.

## Single source of geometry

- Everything visible in the 3D space must trace back to `job.json` (space, cabinets, later boards).
- **Step one is defining the space.** `job.space` is `{ kind, params }` resolved by `renderer/spaces.js` into `floor` polygon + `height` + `obstacles`; the renderer and interaction only consume that resolved shape. Today `kind` is `box`; `vehicle` and `floorplan` plug in as new kinds, never as special cases in the renderer. Redefining the space never moves cabinets - checks report the ones that no longer fit.
- Cabinet geometry comes from parametric generators (`moduleId + params` → boards). The TypeScript sources live in `the-cab-lab/generators/*/generator.ts` and belong only to The Cab Lab. Reuse these sources; never re-implement cabinet formulas in the renderer. The renderer imports them as esbuild bundles in `renderer/gen/` (`npm run build:generators`); never edit the bundles.
- A cabinet's box (envelope) **is** its outer-size params. `renderer/modules.js` maps envelope W/D/H and divider handles onto each module's params; there is no separate envelope record in `job.json`.
- **Three layers** (`docs/model-spec.md`, types in `generators/_lib/model.ts`): module (模块层, `job.cabinets[i]`) → board (板件层, `boards[]`, one piece of stock with a stable role id) → face (面层, `board.faces[]`: `A` = +thicknessAxis side, `B` = − side, `E<i>` = one per outline edge). The outline is the geometric truth — tongues / notches stay in the outline and edge faces only tag them; grooves / holes / cutouts are `FaceFeature`s on A / B in face-local (u, v). Colour, visibility, machining and joints hang on faces, never on the module. Face ids are geometric; `semantic` is annotation only. Only the module layer is stored in `job.json`.
- **Provenance and rules** (`docs/bench-spec.md`). Every board face and outline point a Cab Lab generator emits goes through `dim()` (`generators/_lib/dim.ts`) so `debug.provenance` can say `359 = H - TCH - 1` with each term's kind (param / rule / ref / local). Rule constants (workshop numbers such as T3 depth 90) live in `generators/<module>/rules.json` with a `doc`, wrapped by `rules.ts`; formulas stay in code; bare literals stay literals. Expected values are **pins** in `generators/<module>/presets.json`, asserted by the generator test — do not hand-write board coordinates in tests. A generator joins the bench when it has provenance + presets; a bundle that only mimics `boards` through a renderer adapter does not.
- Editing = change `job.json` / `params` / zones / board attributes, then regenerate. Undo = previous `job.json` snapshot, not a feature timeline.
- **Colour is a job-level rule, not a module setting.** Door colour, series (Acrylic / HPL) and single / double sides live in the job catalogue (`job.finish`), in groups (door A / B today; later e.g. upper cabinets vs lower). Today new cabinets copy the group's values at creation. When changing colours mid-job is added, a cabinet stores only its group (`colorSlot`) and the values are filled in at generation time: changing a group's rule regenerates every cabinet and board in that group — colour, sides, everything — with no cabinet left on the old value.
- **No physical overlap, ever.** Two solids (partition walls, door leaves / pelmets, cabinets, space walls) may touch, never intersect — there is no "legal" overlap such as a T-joint buried in another wall. Centre lines and reference faces exist for **alignment and annotation only**: a point may be picked on a partition's centre line, but the board that results stops on that partition's **face** (`walls.js trimToFaces`); a sliding door's pelmet stops on the next partition's face; cabinets stop at every solid box. Any overlap that survives is an error the checks must report (`overlaps …`), never a case to special-case away.

## Keep computation small

The app must stay fast in a browser on an ordinary machine (it may be served from the web). Every edit regenerates only the cabinet it touched (`job.resultFor` caches per cabinet); a generator run stays under `GENERATE_BUDGET_MS` (5 ms, asserted in `generators/_lib/smoke.test.ts`; today < 1 ms). The 3D view rebuilds only cabinets whose inputs changed (`cabinets3d.js buildKey`) and disposes the geometry it drops. Never add work that runs for every cabinet on every pointer move.

## 3D layer (Three.js / renderer) only displays

- Draw boards as boxes / outlines from generator output. Do not build solids, booleans, sketches, or constraint solvers in the renderer.
- Never edit mesh vertices to change a cabinet. If a size must change, change a parameter.
- Selection, drag-move, rotate 90°, snapping to walls or neighbours are allowed; they only mutate `pose`.

## Units and axes (fixed)

- Internal unit is **millimetres** everywhere. Never mix metres.
- **Z is up**, XY is the floor, right-handed. Grid: 100 mm minor, 1000 mm major.
- **Origin is the front-left floor corner of the space**: X to the right, Y toward the back wall, Z up. Cabinet local origin is the front carcass face at floor level (fronts sit at negative local Y). Importers (vehicle bodies, floor plans) must convert into this convention.

## Scope guard

Do not add: physics engine, BRep/OCCT kernel, an external CAD API, CAM/toolpaths, nesting inside the renderer. Manufacturing (outlines → nesting → labels) consumes the same `boards` data downstream, outside the 3D layer.

## App shell

- Electron shell: `main.js` (window), `renderer/` (scene). CSP allows the inline import map only by sha256 hash - recompute the hash if the import map text changes.
- `ensure-electron.js` repairs a missing `electron.exe`; `create-desktop-shortcut.ps1` builds `The Cab Lab.exe` + desktop shortcut. Keep both working.
